Bash refusals now count as deterministic refusals, committed as dc11ae62 ("Part of #1068"). When the bash tool refuses a command, the model gets the refusal text unchanged: no "try a simpler version" hint, no bump to the failure counter, and the whole prompt is no longer re-run automatically. Build, all tests, clippy and the fmt check passed.

I also hit the bug live during this session. A command I ran contained rm -rf / inside a script, the hard-deny guard blocked it, and the reply carried the exact hint this task removes.

What the bash tool can refuse with. I searched for every refusal message it can return. Five message beginnings (stems) cover them all:
• Command blocked by safety policy:  (the hard-deny list in hard_deny.rs)
• Command blocked by your permission rules:  (the user's deny list, plus the #1010/#1011 refusals in safety/indirect_command.rs; the deny list added by /cd uses the same text)
• Command needs user confirmation that a sub-agent cannot obtain:  (a sub-agent command that would need the user's confirmation)
• ...git may not be redirected outside it. (git pointed outside a pinned working directory)
• Command was not confirmed by the user. (the user answering no). The task didn't list this one; I added it because it is the bash equivalent of the existing User denied  stem.

Two things are deliberately not counted. --restricted and read-only sub-agents remove tools instead of refusing, so they produce no text. A Blocked by hook:  refusal comes from a user-written hook and can depend on outside state, so it is still treated as a real failure.

Changes
• BASH_REFUSAL_STEMS sits beside the existing REFUSAL_STEM_* consts in src/tool_wrappers.rs, and is_deterministic_tool_error now checks it. The recovery-hint wrapper already uses that same check, so one change covers both the hint and the auto-retry.
• Both files were at their size caps, so the tests went into a new file, src/bash_refusal_tests.rs, and I recorded the new ceilings for both files in tests/module_size.rs:
  • Every refusal is built by calling the real code that produces it.
  • Lookalikes stay classed as real failures: command not found, Exit code: 1, "blocked by the proxy", and a bare "Command blocked".
  • A hard-deny and a user-deny refusal pass through the hint wrapper byte-identical, checked with assert_eq!, and the failure counter stays at 0.
  • A genuine tool error passed through the same wrapper still gets the hint.
• Check that the tests catch a regression: with the new check deleted, 3 of the 4 new tests failed by name. After restoring it, all 4 pass, and no sabotage marker is left in the code.
• Docs: I added a short paragraph to docs/src/configuration/permissions.md. That page, not safety.md, is where refusals were described.

I posted a comment on #1068 listing the stems that are now covered and noting that defect 2 is still open. The hard_deny.rs doc says a quoted string counts as one word, but multi-line quoted arguments are split into lines first; it fails closed.
